e [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343299/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "402e4563-e5e3-5183-bae8-fdf43d8669be" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-343299/field-3/a8a92ea07ac7485e9fd6d2e4cc37ff1cfe2285f70b07eaedfdf36fc578c9e108" :answer-field/choices ["q-343299/field-3/a8a92ea07ac7485e9fd6d2e4cc37ff1cfe2285f70b07eaedfdf36fc578c9e108" "q-343299/field-3/def9e284c04ff88f4501b741fdfd679144aa2f6e42b9c1ec7d4a2217819cc46c" "q-343299/field-3/4ddf0edc896c1f1ae6c38e2226560fa57e92098045c3c07394f525b12ebbd5f4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-343299" :question/id #uuid "fcef6993-aa47-5f09-895e-379cbe321c35" :question/math-academy-id "q-343299" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{3}{x}{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(0,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(2)=5.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(2)=5,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{3}{x}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{3}{x}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{3}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{x}^{-3} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =\\frac{C}{{2}^{3}} \\\\ 5 & =\\frac{C}{8} \\\\ C & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x>0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-343299/field-1" "q-343299/field-2" "q-343299/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-343299"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186216643 :answer-field/correct 17592186216647 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342724/field-2/647155aec3613341a351e0ad8f72de15810b769f8f2cd6428995e0c87651cf0a" :answer/id #uuid "c38e73e2-aece-5726-acc3-fb6e1b3a841c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Cx" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342724/field-2/1c8d0a3a4371dfddfd2cf3fd36a8ff51c401c07b199ef9aa64078d346e456e14" :answer/id #uuid "7cd55b4e-6889-5254-9250-7cadc86fa9d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C{e}^{-2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342724/field-2/38c038a4d1d1a1830ad1e16cd106a1083e3864c90c66b443e991f3f39e470a71" :answer/id #uuid "b051053d-d286-531a-876b-d04e53a1db5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342724/field-2/13e8896011340eadf33c4d524929dee7edcf555d4713d91c82dc9a74fe98be2b" :answer/id #uuid "2332cbc2-c380-52cb-a351-45c5ded32e46" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C{e}^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342724/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "9612ebd1-4dee-53f1-aa10-a05b110b82a6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-342724/field-2/13e8896011340eadf33c4d524929dee7edcf555d4713d91c82dc9a74fe98be2b" :answer-field/choices ["q-342724/field-2/647155aec3613341a351e0ad8f72de15810b769f8f2cd6428995e0c87651cf0a" "q-342724/field-2/1c8d0a3a4371dfddfd2cf3fd36a8ff51c401c07b199ef9aa64078d346e456e14" "q-342724/field-2/38c038a4d1d1a1830ad1e16cd106a1083e3864c90c66b443e991f3f39e470a71" "q-342724/field-2/13e8896011340eadf33c4d524929dee7edcf555d4713d91c82dc9a74fe98be2b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342724/field-3/e3cf2efbe1b243991dad4797fc800da87c7458d44af17da132ec499bd6595b28" :answer/id #uuid "197784a1-20a0-5079-9cd2-1a8bd2e757c4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for some but not all $x\\in R$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342724/field-3/d2f4a8d7bf19ba91ffcfe3f2bf391cb2285df557a172f4d58a990c4e894ee1e1" :answer/id #uuid "15418217-b5a9-5169-a102-869b12624d6b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342724/field-3/08fc3946806b1a30042891f2e1a7c0b3d141b9fbc3c3f65ca96eb5887fe978cb" :answer/id #uuid "e218f341-d01c-52b2-8023-52d230848b21" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342724/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "fe235196-f225-5d31-b709-24f898f4e0ff" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-342724/field-3/08fc3946806b1a30042891f2e1a7c0b3d141b9fbc3c3f65ca96eb5887fe978cb" :answer-field/choices ["q-342724/field-3/e3cf2efbe1b243991dad4797fc800da87c7458d44af17da132ec499bd6595b28" "q-342724/field-3/d2f4a8d7bf19ba91ffcfe3f2bf391cb2285df557a172f4d58a990c4e894ee1e1" "q-342724/field-3/08fc3946806b1a30042891f2e1a7c0b3d141b9fbc3c3f65ca96eb5887fe978cb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-342724"] :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(0)=1.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(0)=1,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=2W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =2W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =2\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =2x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{2x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{2\\cdot 0} \\\\ 1 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)={e}^{2x}$ and $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342724/field-2" "q-342724/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21868"] :question/problem "$$\nx^2y''-5xy'+\\ln(x)y=0, \\qquad x>0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let $y_1$ and $y_2$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in standard form. Since $x>0$, we can divide both sides by $x^2$ to get\n\n$$\ny''-\\frac{5}{x}y'+\\frac{\\ln(x)}{x^2}y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{5}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\nW'(x)= \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\frac{5}{x}W(x)}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21869"] :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+\\tan(x)y'+e^x y=0, \\qquad -\\frac{\\pi}{2} < x < \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(0)=4.$ What is $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)?$" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\tan(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-\\tan(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ W & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=4$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ 4 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=4\\cos x.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21870"] :question/problem "$$\ny''+4y'+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet $y_1$ and $y_2$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty,\\infty).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(2)=-3.$ Use Abel's identity to conclude whether $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent on $\\mathbb{R}.$" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)= \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{-4}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-4W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-4x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-4x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=-3$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{-4\\cdot 2} \\\\ -3 & =C{e}^{-8} \\\\ C & =-3{e}^{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=-3e^{8}e^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nSince $-3e^{8}e^{-4x}\\neq 0$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$, we conclude that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/assets/1e26515be7d7b9ecf5cd5e1464989f6d90098247bdd95dfae75fd2545fd9bd18.png)\n\nSo, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/assets/6397adc2a5f64c899725f85822627697055696c0898ffb6e2ee1d2417e802caa.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
